# Task API

A lightweight, RESTful Express.js API for managing a to-do list, secured with Supabase-backed authentication.

This project has gone through four stages:
1. **In-memory** array (data lost on every restart)
2. **SQLite** via `better-sqlite3` (a local `tasks.db` file — survives restarts, single file)
3. **PostgreSQL in Docker** — a real database server, running in its own container, with the entire stack (app + database) started by a single command
4. **Authentication** (current version) — Supabase Auth handles sign up, log in, and log out, issuing JWTs that protect specific routes

Across all four stages, the core task CRUD routes — every path, every status code, every response shape — stayed the same. Only the storage layer and, now, the security layer changed underneath them.

## Why Supabase Auth

Instead of writing password hashing, token signing, and session management by hand (all of which are easy to get subtly wrong and risky to get wrong at all), this project uses Supabase as an **Identity Provider**. The client sends credentials directly to Supabase; Supabase returns a signed **JWT (JSON Web Token)**; the client then presents that token to this API on every request that needs it; this server verifies the token with Supabase before letting the request through. The server never stores or even sees a raw password.

## Prerequisites

- [Docker Desktop](https://www.docker.com/products/docker-desktop/) or Docker Engine (Linux/WSL2) for Postgres — you do **not** need Postgres installed locally, it runs in a container.
- Node.js, for running the API itself.
- A free [Supabase](https://supabase.com) project.

## Setup & Run

1. Clone this repo.
2. Copy the example environment file and fill in your own values:
   ```
   cp .env.example .env
   ```
3. Start Postgres (via Docker) and the API. For local development:
   ```
   docker start taskdb        # or: docker run ... (see .env.example / Docker guide for the full command)
   npm install
   node index.js
   ```
   You'll see:
   ```
   [db] tasks table already has N row(s) - skipping seed
   server running on port 8000 and connected to supabase
   ```
4. The API is live at `http://localhost:8000`. Interactive docs (with auth support) are at `http://localhost:8000/docs`.

## Environment Variables

See `.env.example` for the full list. All are required:

| Variable | Description | Example |
|---|---|---|
| `DATABASE_URL` | Postgres connection string | `postgres://postgres:dev@localhost:5432/tasks` |
| `SUPABASE_URL` | Your Supabase project URL | `https://xxxxx.supabase.co` |
| `SUPABASE_KEY` | Your Supabase **publishable** key (safe for client-side/low-privilege use — not the secret key) | `sb_publishable_...` |
| `PORT` | Port the Express server listens on | `8000` |

Note: `DATABASE_URL` differs depending on how you run the app — `localhost` for plain `node index.js`, or the service name `db` when running via `docker compose up`.

## Base URL

All endpoints are relative to: `http://localhost:8000`

## API Endpoints

### General & System

| Method | Path | Description | Auth required? |
|---|---|---|---|
| GET | `/` | Basic API info | No |
| GET | `/health` | Server health check | No |
| GET | `/public/info` | Public, unprotected sample data | No |

### Tasks

| Method | Path | Description | Success | Errors | Auth required? |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| GET | `/tasks` | List all tasks | 200 | — | No |
| GET | `/tasks/:id` | Get one task | 200 | 404 if not found | No |
| POST | `/tasks` | Create a task | 201 | 400 if title missing/empty | No |
| PUT | `/tasks/:id` | Update a task (partial) | 200 | 404 if not found, 400 if invalid | No |
| DELETE | `/tasks/:id` | Delete a task | 204 | 404 if not found | No |

### Authentication

| Method | Path | Description | Success | Errors | Auth required? |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| POST | `/auth/signup` | Create a new user account | 201 | 400 if email/password missing or invalid | No |
| POST | `/auth/login` | Log in, returns access & refresh tokens | 200 | 400 if fields missing, 401 if credentials invalid | No |
| POST | `/auth/logout` | Ends the current session | 204 | 401 if token missing/invalid | **Yes** |

### Protected

| Method | Path | Description | Success | Errors | Auth required? |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| GET | `/protected/profile` | Returns the authenticated user's id, email, and creation date | 200 | 401 if token missing, invalid, or expired | **Yes** |
| GET | `/protected/dashboard` | Sample second protected route, demonstrating the same reusable auth middleware | 200 | 401 if token missing, invalid, or expired | **Yes** |

Protected routes expect: `Authorization: Bearer <access_token>`

**Example — signup, login, then a protected request**
```
curl -i -X POST http://localhost:8000/auth/signup -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"email":"you@example.com","password":"yourpassword"}'

curl -i -X POST http://localhost:8000/auth/login -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"email":"you@example.com","password":"yourpassword"}'
# -> returns { "access_token": "...", "refresh_token": "...", "user": {...} }

curl -i http://localhost:8000/protected/profile -H "Authorization: Bearer <paste_access_token_here>"
# -> 200 { "id": "...", "email": "you@example.com", "created_at": "..." }
```

Access tokens expire after a period (commonly 1 hour on Supabase's defaults) — logging in again issues a fresh one.

## Architecture note

All token verification logic lives in a single reusable middleware, `authMiddleware.js`, applied to any route that needs protecting (currently `/auth/logout`, `/protected/profile`, `/protected/dashboard`). Route handlers themselves stay focused on their actual logic — they never re-implement token checking. The Supabase client itself is initialized once, in `supabaseClient.js`, and imported wherever needed (`index.js`, `authMiddleware.js`) rather than recreated per file.

The database connection and all SQL live in `db.js`. The Express routes in `index.js` call `pool.query(...)` directly for now — a dedicated repository/service layer (fully separating routes from SQL) is a later refinement, not part of this stage.

## Swagger UI & Authorization

`http://localhost:8000/docs` shows a lock icon next to every protected route. Click **Authorize**, paste a raw access token (no need to type "Bearer", Swagger adds that automatically), and use **Try it out** to test protected routes directly from the browser.

_Add your Swagger UI screenshot here, showing the lock icons and a successful authorized request to `/protected/profile`._

## Proving persistence (from the Postgres stage)

To confirm data survives a full stack restart (not just an app restart):

1. `docker compose up` — note the seeded tasks via `GET /tasks`
2. Create an extra task via `POST /tasks`
3. `Ctrl+C`, then `docker compose down`
4. `docker compose up` again
5. `GET /tasks` — the extra task is still there, because it lives in the `taskdata` volume, not inside the container itself

